WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:06.500
Anyone who has even a passing interest in esports has probably heard of Lee “Faker” Sang-hyeok.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:15.620
He’s a once-in-a-lifetime talent, a three-time
World Champion, the face of League of Legends,

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.380
and unanimously considered to be the greatest
to ever play the game.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.240
But that wasn’t always the case.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:27.300
In 2013, he was still making a name
for himself. A gifted, yet unproven player

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.840
pulled from the ranks of solo queue
to lead SK Telecom T1K into a new era.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:44.900
(Casting)

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:51.000
However, with OGN broadcasts held behind a
paywall and LoL esports still growing, many

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:55.329
European and North American fans, who had
heard the rumblings from Korea, hadn’t yet

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:57.900
witnessed Faker in action firsthand.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:04.379
But that all changed on September 7th, 2013,
when Faker pulled-off a play that would stun

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.499
his opponent and the world alike.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.240
This is the Faker vs. Ryu Outplay.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.460
It’s the grand finals of the 2013 HOT6iX
Champions Summer and SK Telecom T1K are taking

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.990
on the KT Rolster Bullets.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:22.719
SKT T1K had breezed through their group and the first two rounds of the

00:01:22.719 --> 00:01:25.619
playoffs, only dropping a single game.

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:28.399
But here they found themselves under serious
pressure.

00:01:28.399 --> 00:01:33.459
The KT Rolster Bullets had seized a 2-0 lead in the series,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:40.100
before SKT T1K got on the board with two consecutive
wins of their own to force a deciding game.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.460
(Casting)

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.679
This was it – one last blind pick match to crown the champion.

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:48.270
Faker locked in Zed.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.950
Ryu did the same.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.620
It was game on.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:02:03.500
KT took an early lead, but around the 20
minute mark, the tide began to turn in SKT T1K’s favor.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:11.400
(Casting)

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.820
Within touching distance of his first LCK
title, Faker overstepped

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.820
and Ryu threw down the gauntlet.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:34.340
(Casting)

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:39.610
In one of the most brilliant displays of decision-making
and mechanical skill ever, Faker

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.430
despite starting at a significant disadvantage

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.340
completely outplayed Ryu to win the gutsy
duel.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:52.020
The look on Ryu’s face and the absolute
shock of DoA and MonteCristo told the entire story.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:58.420
(Casting)

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.080
Before we get into the play

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.320
It's important to note that despite his team being massively ahead

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:06.140
Faker's not actually that far ahead of Ryu, at all.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.040
But, the one key item difference is the quicksilver sash

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.940
which, at the time, could cleanse off  the Zed ultimate and it's damage.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.040
The play starts with Faker taking tower shots and Ryu's E, auto attacks,

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.420
and blade of the ruined king active under tower

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:24.880
putting the SKT mid-laner under half health after Ryu's passive hits him.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.860
Faker then uses his own blade of the ruined king active to steal move speed and try to run away.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:34.220
At this point Ryu ignites him, hoping to clean up the kill before Faker gets away.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.220
Faker responds with his ultimate,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.640
making him invulnerable and allowing him to avoid damage from Ryu and his ignite

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.380
Ryu responds with his own deathmark to avoid trading kills.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.320
He then misjudges Faker's movement.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.600
Throwing his shadow upwards instead of down while Faker avoids it by jumping to his shadow downward.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.700
Here, the item difference saves Faker's life.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:00.080
He quicksilver sashes the deathmark and the ignite freeing him from all damage.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.400
He then sneaks in a 'Q', ignite, and auto attack against Ryu

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.120
before dodging Ryu's 'Q' and cancelling Ryu's auto attack with a flash to the left.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.020
The slimmest of margins determines the end of this fight.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:16.120
As Ryu attempts to flash to Faker to secure the kill with an auto attack

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.880
Ryu gets his auto cancelled once more this time by Fakers 'W'

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.180
which he didn't use this entire time.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.860
Faker then uses 'E' to finish Ryu off.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.920
(Casting)

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.880
When the clip hit the web, it exploded.

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:36.590
The global League community marveled at Faker’s
incredible exhibition and the young Korean

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:39.220
star quickly became a household name.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:45.040
You have to understand that for most Western
fans in 2013, seeing the play for the first

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.690
time was a revelation.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:52.680
Sure, the outplay didn’t alter the course
of the game — SKT T1K was already poised

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:56.570
to win, but it did change the course of League
history.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:00.870
Everyone who watched that outplay knew that
they were witnessing greatness and that Faker

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.889
would be a player to keep an eye on for years
to come.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.020
And the unkillable demon king has not disappointed.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:32.540
(Casting)

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.640
Now, six domestic titles and three world championships
later, it seems obvious that Faker was destined

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.150
to become the greatest player of all-time.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:46.200
But it all started with an amazing outplay
that put Ryu and the rest of the world on notice.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:06:03.680
(Casting)

